Hej alla,

Finally ascended a character! After having played the game for several
years off and on, since version 3.2.3, I went an an all-out binge for the
last 3 weeks (with interruptions for final exams), and beat the RNG at its
own game. I'll describe the highlights of my dungeon crawl for your
edification and/or enjoyment.

I think Archaeologists are somewhat underappreciated. = ) The ability to
dig and the intrinsic speed (and later searching) came in IMMENSE handy.
While any character can dig with the right tools, not needing to cycle
weapons was a boon. The searching helped me avoid those blasted fire traps.

No pets, as my kitten was killed in DL 2 by a falling rock. Oh well. No
altars either until DL 16. Oh well. Typographical errors cost me easily 6
potions of full healing, but it came out alright in the end. 

The equipment:

Weapons
e - a blessed rustproof +5 dwarvish mattock named Grond

It never left my hands except to occasionally wield a c corpse. Dealing
deathly blows to mountain nymphs, destroying boulders in the dungeon,
tunneling through hell, breaking petrified monsters to loot their items --
all easily accomplished with the discriminating Archaeologist's weapon of
choice, honed to perfection, enchanted to deadly sharpness, rustproofed for
all-weather endurance, and duly blessed by the Most High.

Armor
The usual (SDSM, cloak of MR, speed boots, etc etc). Nothing special.

S - an uncursed thoroughly burnt +1 pair of leather gloves

...were worn throughout the game as a gesture of humility. 

Rings
b - a cursed ring of invisibility named nazgul (on left hand)

Invisibility is teh rulex0rz. I got it from a felled Nazgul, and what did I
care if it was cursed -- I never planned to remove it! 

Various rings occupied the right ring finger, primarly cycling between
levitation, regeneration, and shock resisitance as needed. I eventually
gained that as an intrinsic though, by feasting on the delicious and
abundant corpse of a slain blue D.

Wands

D - an uncursed wand of cancellation (0:4)
H - an uncursed wand of polymorph (0:1)

I cannot overemphasize how important these two wands were to my survival.
I used teleportation wands to get rid of the eels on Medusa's level, true,
but where there is less water (such as in Rodney's Tower), polymorphing the
krakens into whatever (which usually sinks like a stone) was a better idea.
The cancellation wands were put to good use on the rust monsters,
disenchanters, green slimes, and other selected monsters to make them much
less threatening. When I say "two wands", I mean of course "two types of
wands", as I had  four or five of each by the time it was all over.
I was nearly killed by a minotaur on DL 22, but he suddenly turned into a
gecko, and the enemy host were dismayed.

Tools:

m - an uncursed skeleton key

Yay for being able to lock the door behind me and keep the soldier ants (or
orcs, or trolls, or mind flayers) out! I think the game is vastly more
difficult without finding a key early (although a lockpick is alright too),
because potions tend to get smashed while kicking the box, and recklessly 
destroying doors can come back to haunt you later.

Scrolls:

z - a cursed scroll of genocide

The Wizard of Yendor hits! Double trouble...
Which species would you like to genocide? 
cockatrice
Sent in some cockatrices.
...
You hit the Wizard of Yendor with your corpse! --more--
The Wizard of Yendor turns to stone.

I had piles of other junk, but those were the things that came in the most
handy. I've of course omitted other useful items that everyone has (unicorn
horn, etc).


The events:

Gnomish Mines

I always do the Mines ASAP due to the massive amounts of loot to be had
there. For much of the game, my AC was around -11 due to mithril coat,
dwarvish helm, +2 elven cloak found randomly, and the +2 iron shoes, along
with the intrinisc protection granted by the strangely cooperative priest
of Quetzalcoatl in Minetown. I bet he's gnashing his teeth now that
Camaxtli has gained deserved ascendance over the other gods.  Of course, I
obtained my trusty mattock from a hapless dwarf lord.

Quest

The Minion of Huhetotl gave me a good run for my money, but he went down
because I had potions of full healing and an amulet of life saving, and he
didn't. The Orb struck me as pretty useless, since by that time I had a
cloak of MR and intrinisc telepathy and it's really heavy, but I decided
not to leave it behind just in case. This turned out to be a good idea,
since I hate shopkeepers and traditionally kill every one of them (except
Izchak, rest his soul) as soon as I'm powerful enough. However, I didn't
know that attacking peaceful humans could cause one to lose eir telepathy,
so the Orb was useful later.

Aside from serially killing shopkeepers, I assure you that I used
my powers only for Good.

Medusa

That titan was the closest I came to death in the entire game. Cursed my
stuff, stunned me over and over, and summoned a horde of nasties including
two green Ds, a minotaur (eek), and trolls. It took several trips up and
down the stairs to conquer evil that day. I used /oT on the eels that
grabbed me in the water, but for the most part I just ripped into them with
my mattock.  Took medusa out with the mirror (stumbling about blind and
whacking away didn't seem like it would be much fun).

Castle

I found no musical instruments on the way down, and didn't feel like
looking for any, so I slipped on my ring of levitation and floated over the
moat -- invisibly, stealthily, like a deadly breeze on a cold castle day --
and took the soldiery by surprise. 

Hell

Let me begin by saying that I encountered a Lich on DL 11 somehow, and
having half my inventory cursed and my mithril disintegrated motivated me
to genocide them and all their kin as soon as I found the first scroll.
However, that was all I genocided. The mind flayers were no picnic, but
they were tame compared to monsters that could actually kill me like
minotaurs and Jabberwocks.

Digging between the staircases in each maze level helped out a lot on the
way back up. 

Demons

Only Orcus posed any real challenge. He summoned Yeenoghu, then ran away
and skulked about. It took our Hero a very long time to find him again. 

The Wizard's Tower

Rodney was easily the worst thing I had to deal with in my entire game. I
was never able to kill him by direct combat (not once!) and had to resort
to the c corpse or the wand of death. I had a c corpse going into his
tower, but he of course resurrected incessantly, and stole the Amulet (and
the Orb) multiple times.

I piss on the Wizard of Yendor and all his seed for 10,000 generations.

Elemental Planes

Camaxtli forgive me, but on the Plane of Earth I used wands of digging to
tunnel about. I had 7 or 8 of them, and chipping at the filthy stone with
Grond was slow going and was generating a lot of earth elementals, some of
whom were peaceful and kept getting in the way.

The plane of Fire was tricky, due to being constantly surrounded by
relatively powerful monsters. Potions of full healing helped, but my 4th
(and last) amulet of life saving was used up here. 

Astral Plane

Despite putting on the ring of regeneration, this level was tough. I hurled
aloft Grond, the Hammer of the Underworld, but the Angels of Huhetotl were
numerous, and their long swords glittered in the dim light like a forest of
razors. I was surrounded and ready to go down fighting, but then...

The renegade priest of Camaxtli reads a scroll of create monster!  
...
You destroy the cockatrice!  
n - a cockatrice corpse.  
...  
The priest of Camaxtli intones, "Beware, pilgrim, for you enter a sacred
place!"

And so it went. 

-- Jeeves, who now sits at the right hand of the mighty Camaxtli.

